--- Finding (currying): a function held in a record's field, called through the
--- field (`b.f[b.x]`), runs interpreted and does not build compiled: the emitter
--- prints Rust's method-call form `b.f(b.x)`, which looks for a method `f`, where a
--- field holding a function is called as `(b.f)(b.x)`.
---
--- crow run -i: prints
---     4
---     9
--- crow run: the program does not build:
---     error[E0599]: no method named `f` found for struct `Box` in the current scope
---       --> src/main.rs:52:38
---     error: the compiled program does not build — the Rust compiler refused what
---     Crow accepted, a bug in Crow, not in this program (`crow run -i` runs it meanwhile)
---   (`crow build --rs`: `crow_runtime::display::log(&[&(b.f(b.x)) as …])`)
--- Expected: 4 and 9 in both modes.
---
--- The direct call `square[2]` is there so the function is typed (without it both
--- modes refuse, as findings/closures_value_capture-1.co records); the call through
--- a local, `g = b.f` then `g[b.x]`, builds and prints 9 in both modes, so this is
--- the emitter's form for a call whose callee is a field, not the typing. The same
--- in a function taking the record (`fn apply_box[b]` / `return b.f[b.x]`), while a
--- function read from a map (`ops['d'][3]`) or a list (`fs[1][4]`) and called at once
--- builds and agrees. Found
--- writing rosetta/currying.co (a curried function as a record of the function and
--- its first argument), which calls through a local instead.
struct Box [f, x]

fn square[i]
    return i * i

log[square[2]]
b = Box[square, 3]
log[b.f[b.x]]
